// schema.kb — the type of each predicate's fields, in the order a fact writes them.
//
//   predicate(field type, field type)
//
// A type is
//
//   number    a number, as every age is
//   name      any text, which is what a person or a word is here
//   a|b|c     one of these names, exactly
//
// The prompt holds a typed fact against this before storing it, so has-age(tim, twenty)
// is refused rather than filed away where no rule could ever reach it. A predicate the
// file does not name is accepted as it is written: the schema is what the program knows
// about its own facts, not a gate it has to keep widening.
//
// Only the predicates that store facts are listed. A rule's head is defined by the
// conditions in its body, and a comparison such as ?n <= 18 keeps its own field a
// number without being told.
//
// knowledge.ms reads this file; schema.ms loads it for main.ms.

parent-of(name, name)
married-to(name, name)
has-gender(name, male|female)
has-age(name, number)
